\begin_layout Standard
While often subsumed under the term 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

machine learning,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 this modern approach to poverty mapping actually consists of several practices
 that differ from more traditional approaches.
 Most obviously, the modern approach substitutes non-parametric statistical
 methods for the parametric approaches traditionally used for poverty mapping.
 In addition, the modern approach relies heavily on remotely-sensed covariates
 rather than covariates derived from census data.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Nevertheless, traditional area-level metods have made considerable use of
 remotely sensed covariates and often rely on using data available at the
 area-level, including administrative data (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "seitz2019they"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for an application using publicly available geospatial data).
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Finally, poverty maps produced with machine-learning methods generally
 share a common validation procedure.
 The key feature of this procedure is that model performance is assessed
 by calculating the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 from a regression of the observed poverty measures on the estimated poverty
 measures for the same geographical units.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
For examples of the above procedure, see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "jean2016combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pokhriyal2017combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "steele2017mapping"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "lee2020high"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yeh2020using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, or 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chi2022microestimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Note that a few studies use a similar procedure, but fit the machine-learning
 model to household-level data and then aggregate the model's predictions
 to the geographic unit of interest 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "blumenstock2015predicting,hersh2021open,aiken2022machine"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The poverty measures used in this procedure are most often direct, sample-based
 estimates rather than the true poverty measures for the regions of interest.
 While direct estimates are unbiased, they can be imprecise estimates of
 true poverty measures, meaning that it is unclear whether the validation
 procedure is informative of actual model performance 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "rodas2021pull"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.

\change_inserted 428888767 1711444841
 In addition, we are often interested in how well the predictions perform
 in all areas, not on aggregate.
 Consequently, the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 measure hides the heterogeneity across areas.
 
\change_unchanged

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this paper, we provide a more rigorous assessment of the performance
 of the modern approach to poverty mapping.
 Our approach consists of constructing a pseudo-census (hereafter 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

census
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) that we use to conduct several simulation experiments.
 Our experiments entail repeatedly drawing survey samples from the census
 where each sample produces direct poverty estimates for the sampled geographic
 areas.
 With each sample we obtain small area estimates using traditional and machine
 learning based methods.
 The presence of the census means that we observe the ``true'' poverty measure
 corresponding to the direct estimate.
 We use these true poverty measures to gain insight not only into the credibilit
y of model validation based on direct estimates, but also the performance
 of machine-learning methods when validated against the true poverty measures.
 Given that population censuses rarely collect detailed data on income or
 expenditures, we construct our census from a large-scale household survey
 conducted in Mexico: the Mexican Intercensal Survey of 2015.

\change_inserted 428888767 1711443878
 The Mexican Intracensal Survey represents our population and it contains
 an income measure that yields the true poverty values that we wish to estimate.
\change_unchanged

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_inserted 428888767 1711443922
In contrast to other papers which focus on the 
