\begin_layout Standard
The Mexican Intercensal Survey was carried out by the Mexican National Institute
 of Statistics and Geography (INEGI).
 The sample consists of 5.9 million households and is representative at the
 national, state (32 states), and municipality level (2,457 municipalities).
 It is also representative for localities with populations of 50,000 or
 more inhabitants.
 The administered questionnaire gathered information on household income,
 geographic location, household demographics, dwelling characteristics,
 and economic activities, among others.
 The size of the dataset is especially important, as it allows us to draw
 repeated samples that are sufficiently large and diverse.
 In addition, the fact that the survey gathered detailed income information
 on all households allows us to 
\change_deleted 428888767 1712734079
reliably 
\change_unchanged
calculate the required poverty measures that serve as the basis for our
 simulation experiments.
\change_inserted 428888767 1712734341

\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\change_inserted 428888767 1712734341
Income is defined as money received from work performed during the course
 of the reference period by individuals aged 12 or older.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The intercensus is used as the reference population and the poverty rates
 from the intercensus are used as the true poverty rates that are to be
 estimated.
\change_deleted 428888767 1712734321

\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Income is defined as money received from work performed during the course
 of the reference period by individuals aged 12 or older.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\change_unchanged
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Prior to sampling from the Intercensal Survey, we make three modifications
 to the data.
 First, as a large number of households reported earning no income, we randomly
 remove 90 percent of these households.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This is done to ensure some missing values are present in the data used,
 but not as many as in the original data.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Second, to ensure that all municipalities are sufficiently large, municipalitie
s with less than 500 households are removed.
 Finally, to ensure that all primary sampling units (PSUs) are also sufficiently
 large for sampling purposes, we combine several neighboring PSUs such that
 all include at least 300 households.
 Our final census dataset consists of 3.9 million households in 1,865 municipalit
ies and 16,297 PSUs.
 The resulting census is used to draw 500 survey samples that serve as the
 basis for our 
\change_inserted 428888767 1712734458
design-based 
\change_unchanged
simulation experiments (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealp
key "tzavidis2018start"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 In what follows, we describe our approach to constructing these samples.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our sampling procedure is intended to reflect standard design elements used
 in many face-to-face household surveys conducted in the developing world
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "grosh1996manual"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Mexico's 32 states comprise the intended domains of the sample and the
 indicator of interest is household income per capita.
 For each sample, we seek to achieve a relative standard error (RSE) of
 7.5 percent for average household income in each state.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The desired precision of 7.5 percent is somewhat arbitrary, but corresponds
 to precision targets in similar surveys and yields samples of reasonable
 sizes.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 We use a two-stage clustered design wherein PSUs or clusters are selected
 within each domain and then a sample of 10 households is selected within
 each cluster.
 Our decision to select 10 households within each cluster is consistent
 with the design of other large-scale household surveys conducted in developing
 countries.
 Under simple random sampling, the minimum sample size required for each
 state is as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
n_{s}=\left(\frac{\sigma_{s}}{\bar{{w}}_{s}\times\text{{RSE}}}\right)^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{{w}_{s}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{s}$
\end_inset

 represent average household income per capita and the standard deviation
 of per capita income for state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, respectively.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The minimum sample size under simple random sampling must be adjusted to
 account for the clustered design.
 The design effect due to clustering is accounted for by estimating the
 intra-cluster correlation of per capita income within each state.
 The correlation estimates can then be used to adjust the above sample sizes
 for the clustered design as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
n_{s}\times\Big[1+\rho_{s}\left(n_{sc}-1\right)\Big]
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{sc}$
\end_inset

 denotes the number of households selected in cluster 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 within state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 (10 in this case) and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{s}$
\end_inset

 is the intra-cluster correlation of per capita income.
 Given the above, we can calculate the number of clusters needed to achieve
 the minimum sample size for each state and then multiply this number by
 10 to find the final household sample size.
 Taking these sample size requirements as given, each of our samples is
 then drawn in accordance with the two-stage design.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Specifically, the clusters within each state are selected with probability
 proportional to size (without replacement), where the measure of size is
 the number of households within each cluster.
 The households within each cluster are then selected via simple random
 sampling.
 According to this design, the inclusion probability for a given household
 is then approximately:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\tilde{{n}}_{sc}N_{s}}{\tilde{{n}}_{s}}\times\frac{n_{sc}}{\tilde{{n}}_{sc}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{{n}}_{sc}$
\end_inset

 is the total number of households in cluster 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 within state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{s}$
\end_inset

 is the number of clusters selected in state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{{n}}_{s}$
\end_inset

 is the total number of households in state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The equation used to calculate inclusion probabilities assumes sampling
 with replacement, but is used here as an approximation of inclusion probabiliti
es under proportional selection without replacement.
 This should provide a reasonable approximation in this case since there
 are a relatively large number of clusters present in the frame.
 The design weight for each household is simply the inverse of the inclusion
 probability.
 In a typical survey, the design weights would be further adjusted for nonrespon
se and calibrated to known population characteristics.
 However, since the sampling is only a simulation exercise, there is no
 nonresponse and thus no nonresponse adjustment is required.
 Calibration or post-stratification could be performed, but was not implemented
 to simplify the process.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The sample size across the 500 samples is roughly 23,500 households.
 Under this sampling scenario, not all municipalities are included, and
 the number of municipalities included varies from sample to sample, ranging
 between 951 to 1,020 municipalities.
 The median municipality included in a given sample is represented by a
 single cluster and thus its sample size is 10 households.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition to the Intercensal Survey, INEGI has also released geo-referenced
 data that is similar to the remotely-sensed data often used in machine-learning
 applications.
 These geographic variables were calculated by INEGI for the year 2015 (the
 same year as the Intercensal Survey) and are only available at the municipality
 level.
 The geo-referenced data includes 105 different indicators.
 Specifically, the dataset includes information from 21 different dimensions
 and each dimension is associated with five different summary measures.
 For example, we summarize the atmospherically resistant vegetation index
 for each municipality using the minimum, maximum, mean, sum, and standard
 deviation of the index.
 The same procedure is applied to all dimensions.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The 21 dimensions are as follows: enhanced vegetation index, normalized
 difference vegetation index, normalized difference built-up index, built-up
 index, simple ratio, atmospherically resistant vegetation index, urban
 index, index of biological integrity, normalized difference water index,
 modified normalized difference water index, new built-up index, band ratio
 for built-up area, normalized built-up area index, built-up area extraction
 index, normalized difference snow index, visible atmospherically resistant
 index, soil-adjusted vegetation index, optimized soil-adjusted vegetation
 index, normalized difference moisture index, digital altitude model, and
 digital slope model.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 We then have two alternative sets of covariates that we use in our simulations:
 the geo-referenced data and the standard set of sociodemographic characteristic
s from the census.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Methods
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:methods"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section describes the methods we apply to the Mexican Intercensal Survey.
 The first subsection discusses machine learning, with a special focus on
 gradient boosting, as it is among the most popular machine-learning methods
 for poverty mapping.
 The second subsection then discusses more traditional approaches to poverty
 mapping, which we use as the benchmark in our performance assessment of
 the machine-learning methods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Machine learning
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The goal of machine learning is to develop high-performance algorithms for
 prediction, classification, and clustering/grouping tasks 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "varian2014big,athey2018impact,athey2019machine"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Such tasks can be divided into supervised and unsupervised learning problems.
 While unsupervised learning focuses on identifying clusters of observations
 that are similar in terms of their features, supervised learning uses a
 set of features to predict some outcome of interest.
 Supervised learning can be further divided into regression and classification
 tasks, where regression is concerned with predicting continuous outcomes
 and classification focuses on categorical outcomes.
 While there are many machine-learning methods used for regression and classific
ation tasks (e.g., lasso, random forests, or support vector machines), here
 we fix ideas by focusing on a method that has been particularly popular
 for poverty mapping: gradient boosting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Originally developed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "friedman2001greedy"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, gradient boosting machines are a family of machine-learning techniques
 that combine a large number of weak learners to form a stronger ensemble
 prediction.
 Unlike some common ensemble techniques (e.g., random forests) that simply
 average models in the ensemble, gradient boosting adds models to the ensemble
 sequentially by fitting new weak learners to the negative gradient of some
 chosen loss function.
 With the classic squared-error loss function, this amounts to sequentially
 fitting new models to the current residuals of the ensemble.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "natekin2013gradient"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for an accessible introduction to gradient boosting.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost) is a particularly popular implementation
 of gradient boosting that uses classification and regression trees as the
 base models or weak learners.
 The popularity of XGBoost is due to the fact that it is fast, scalable,
 and has been shown to outperform competing methods across a wide range
 of problems 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "chen2016xgboost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Classification and regression trees are based on a sequential binary splitting
 of the covariate space that serves to partition the data into subsamples
 that are increasingly homogeneous in terms of the outcome variable.
 A tree begins with a single root node that is split into two child nodes
 according to some decision rule.
 A decision rule pertains to a single explanatory variable and observations
 are assigned to the child nodes depending on whether they meet some condition
 associated with the explanatory variable.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
For example, for a continuous explanatory variable, one determines whether
 a given observation falls above or below some numeric cutoff.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The child nodes can be further split into new child nodes based on different
 decision rules that involve other explanatory variables and cutoff points.
 Any child node that is not further split is referred to as a terminal node
 or leaf, and each leaf is associated with a parameter that provides a predictio
n for the subsample associated with that leaf.
 For any given observation, the ensemble prediction used by XGBoost is the
 sum of that observation's predictions across all trees in the ensemble.

\change_inserted 428888767 1712734936
 For an in-depth discussion and description of the XGBoost algorithm refer
 to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chen2016xgboost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\change_unchanged

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712734966
In what follows, we discuss the key features of the XGBoost algorithm, drawing
 on the presentation provided in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chen2016xgboost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}$
\end_inset

 denote the outcome of interest (e.g., direct estimates of poverty rates)
 for observation or entity 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 denote a vector of explanatory variables.
 In line with the above, the predicted value of the outcome 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{p}$
\end_inset

 is given by the sum of predictions across the individual trees in the ensemble:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712734966
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{p}=\sum_{t}f_{t}(x_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the trees are indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{t}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 gives the prediction of a given tree as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 To build the trees used in the model, XGBoost minimizes the following regulariz
ed objective function:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712734966
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}=\sum_{i}l(y_{i}^{d},y_{i}^{p})+\sum_{t}r(f_{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $l(y_{i}^{d},y_{i}^{p})$
\end_inset

 is the loss associated with the 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{th}$
\end_inset

 observation and 
\begin_inset Formula $r(f_{t})$
\end_inset

 is a regularization term that serves to mitigate overfitting.
 In our implementation, we use the classic squared-error loss such that
 
\begin_inset Formula $l(y_{i}^{d},y_{i}^{p})=(y_{i}^{d}-y_{i}^{p})^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712734966
XGBoost uses the following specification of the regularization term: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712734966
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r(f_{t})=\gamma M_{t}+\frac{1}{2}\lambda\sum_{j}m_{tj}^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the total number of leaves in tree 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{tj}$
\end_inset

 is the prediction associated with the 
\begin_inset Formula $j^{th}$
\end_inset

 leaf in a given tree, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 are hyperparameters that must be chosen by the user (we discuss our selection
 of hyperparameters below).
 The regularization term serves to mitigate overfitting by penalizing complicate
d trees and smoothing the predictions associated with each tree.
 That is, the method relies on a large number of weak learners and the regulariz
ation term helps control the complexity of the learners.
 It is important to note that the above specification of the regularization
 term is only one of many possible specifications, though 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chen2016xgboost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 claim that it is simpler than the leading alternatives and easier to paralleliz
e.
 Further note that when the hyperparameters are set to zero, the objective
 function becomes equivalent to that used in traditional gradient boosting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712734966
To minimize the objective function, the model is trained in a sequential
 manner, building one tree at a time.
 The objective function at iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 can be written as follows: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712734966
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{t}=\sum_{i}l(y_{i}^{d},y_{i,t-1}^{p}+f_{t}(x_{i}))+r(f_{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i,t}^{p}=y_{i,t-1}^{p}+f_{t}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Each iteration then adds the tree 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{t}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 that greedily minimizes the objective function at that iteration.
 More specifically, XGBoost uses a second-order Taylor series expansion
 to approximate the loss function at any given iteration:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712734966
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{t}=\sum_{i}\left\{ g(y_{i}^{d},y_{i,t-1}^{p})f_{t}(x_{i})+\frac{1}{2}h(y_{i}^{d},y_{i,t-1}^{p})f_{t}(x_{i})^{2}\right\} +r(f_{t})\label{eq:approx}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\cdot,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\cdot,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 denote the first- and second-order derivatives of the loss function 
\begin_inset Formula $l(y_{i}^{d},y_{i,t-1}^{p})$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i,t-1}^{p}$
\end_inset

.
 That is, the point 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i,t-1}^{p}$
\end_inset

 is taken as the basis for the expansion.
 The above conveniently omits the first term of the series 
\begin_inset Formula $l(y_{i}^{d},y_{i,t-1}^{p})$
\end_inset

 because it is a constant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712734966
The XGBoost algorithm is based on a re-expression of Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:approx"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{j}$
\end_inset

 denote the set of observations that belong to the 
\begin_inset Formula $j^{th}$
\end_inset

 leaf of the tree being built at iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Substituting in the explicit form of the regularization term and noting
 that all observation that belong to the same leaf receive the same prediction
 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{tj}$
\end_inset

, we can write 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712734966
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{t}=\sum_{j}\sum_{i\in z_{j}}\left\{ g(y_{i}^{d},y_{i,t-1}^{p})m_{tj}+\frac{1}{2}h(y_{i}^{d},y_{i,t-1}^{p})m_{tj}^{2}\right\} +\gamma M_{t}+\frac{1}{2}\lambda\sum_{j}m_{tj}^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

or, equivalently,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712734966
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{t}=\sum_{j}\left\{ G_{j}m_{tj}+\frac{1}{2}(H_{j}+\lambda)m_{tj}^{2}\right\} +\gamma M_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{j}$
\end_inset

 represent the sums over 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\cdot,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\cdot,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 for all observations in 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the summation in the above is over 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

, which indexes the leaves in the tree.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712734966
For a given tree structure, we can find the optimal prediction values by
 minimizing the objective function with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{tj}$
\end_inset

, which yields 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{tj}^{*}=-G_{j}/(H_{j}+\lambda)$
\end_inset

.
 With a squared-error loss function, where 
\begin_inset Formula $g(y_{i}^{d},y_{i,t-1}^{p})=-2(y_{i}^{d}-y_{i,t-1}^{p})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h(y_{i}^{d},y_{i,t-1}^{p})=2$
\end_inset

, the optimal prediction values are effectively the (penalized) average
 of the residuals associated with a given leaf.
 We can then substitute the optimal prediction values into the objective
 function to obtain the following: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712734966
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{t}^{*}=-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{j}\frac{G_{j}^{2}}{H_{j}+\lambda}+\gamma M_{t}\,,
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which gives us the minimal objective function for a given tree structure.
 Ideally, one would enumerate all possible tree structures and then use
 the tree associated with the smallest value of the (minimized) objective
 function, but this is often intractable.
 XGBoost instead implements an alternative approach that seeks to minimize
 the objective at a given iteration by greedily optimizing one level of
 the tree at a time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712734966
To this end, consider splitting a node in a given tree into 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

left
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

right
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 child nodes based on some candidate splitting rule.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{R}$
\end_inset

 represent 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{j}$
\end_inset

 for the left and right child nodes, respectively.
 Similarly, let 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{R}$
\end_inset

 represent 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{j}$
\end_inset

 for the child nodes.
 We can then find the reduction in the objective function as follows: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712734966
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta\tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{t}^{*}=\frac{1}{2}\left\{ \frac{G_{L}^{2}}{H_{L}+\lambda}+\frac{G_{R}^{2}}{H_{R}+\lambda}-\frac{(G_{L}+G_{R})^{2}}{H_{L}+H_{R}+\lambda}\right\} -\gamma\label{eq:gain}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the first two terms in parentheses capture the loss associated with
 the child nodes and the third term captures the loss associated with the
 parent node being split.
 When splitting a node in any given tree, XGBoost then evaluates Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:gain"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) for every possible split rule for every available explanatory variable
 and chooses the split associated with the maximal reduction in the objective
 function.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This is known as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

exact greedy algorithm.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 XGBoost also has an approximate algorithm that can be used for large datasets.
 See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chen2016xgboost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for details.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 XGBoost then continues to split nodes in a given tree until a stopping
 criterion is met (e.g., a user-specified maximum tree depth).
 Once a given tree is built, XGBoost then moves to building the next tree
 in the ensemble and continues to do so until reaching the user-specified
 number of trees to build.
\change_unchanged

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712735273
The above references
\change_inserted 428888767 1712735283
XGBoost requires the specification of
\change_unchanged
 several hyperparameters that must be chosen by the user, including the
 hyperparameters in the regularization term
\change_deleted 428888767 1712735419
 (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

)
\change_unchanged
, the maximum tree depth, and the number of trees in the ensemble.
 There are two additional hyperparameters that can be used to further prevent
 overfitting.
 First, one can use column or feature subsampling where each tree in the
 ensemble is built based on a (uniformly) random subset of all explanatory
 variables.
 This procedure can also speed up the algorithm because it reduces the number
 of covariates that must be searched across when creating new splits.
 Second, one can use shrinkage by scaling the predictions associated with
 each tree by a factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta$
\end_inset

, which is often called the learning rate.
 The predicted value of the outcome at iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is then given by 
\change_inserted 428888767 1712735585
the sum of the previous prediction and the current prediction scaled by
 the learning rate
\change_deleted 428888767 1712735590

\begin_inset Formula $y_{i,t}^{p}=y_{i,t-1}^{p}+\eta f_{t}(x_{i})$
\end_inset


\change_unchanged
.
 Shrinkage reduces the influence of each tree so that no individual tree
 has too much influence on the ensemble prediction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One possible approach to hyperparameter selection is to use a grid search
 where the user (1) specifies the domain of the hyperparameters, (2) applies
 the model to every combination of hyperparameters, and (3) selects the
 hyperparameters that perform the best in terms of some metric (e.g., mean
 squared prediction error in the validation dataset).
 Grid search, however, is computationally inefficient because hyperparameter
 proposals are uninformed by previous evaluations.
 We instead use a Bayesian hyperparameter optimization procedure, which
 sequentially updates a probability model that relates the hyperparameter
 space to the performance metric and then uses the model to intelligently
 explore the hyperparameter space 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "bergstra2011algorithms"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Specifically, our implementation of gradient boosting relies on the open-source
 library XGBoost, which we combine with the Hyperopt library that conducts
 Bayesian hyperparameter optimization using the tree-structured Parzen estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally, regarding our application to the Mexican data, recall that mapping
 procedures entail an estimation stage and a prediction stage.
 For the machine-learning methods, we perform this procedure at two different
 levels of analysis, namely the PSU and municipality levels.
 Our focus is nevertheless on obtaining estimates of poverty rates at the
 municipality level, meaning that when we conduct estimation and prediction
 at the PSU level, we further aggregate the predictions to the municipality
 level using population-weighted averaging.
 Further, recall that we have two alternative sets of covariates available,
 including geo-referenced and census-based variables.
 Given that the geo-referenced data is only available at the municipality
 level, we conduct estimation and prediction at the municipality level for
 any implementation that uses these covariates.
 When using census-based covariates, however, we consider implementations
 at both the PSU and municipality levels.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In all cases, we pass every eligible variable to the machine-learning model
 and let the algorithm conduct variable selection.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Traditional methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We consider three traditional poverty-mapping methods as a way to benchmark
 the performance of the machine-learning approach.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
For a review of small area estimation methods, see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "rao2015small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 For a review of methods in the context of poverty mapping, see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "molina2022estimation"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pfeffermann2013new"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Specifically, we consider area-level models, unit-level models, and unit-contex
t models.
 First proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "fay1979estimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, area-level models conduct estimation and prediction using data aggregated
 to the geographic area of interest (e.g., the PSU or municipality level).
 The area-level model consists of two basic parts.
 The first part of the model links the actual or true poverty rates 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 for the 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{th}$
\end_inset

 entity to a vector of area-level covariates 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{a}=x_{i}\beta+\varepsilon_{i}\label{eq:linking-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is a vector of parameters to be estimated and 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents random area-level effects that are assumed to be mean zero with
 a constant variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Since we lack information on the true poverty rates, this model cannot
 be immediately fit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Instead, direct estimates of poverty rates from survey data 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}$
\end_inset

 are used as the outcome variable.
 These estimates are nevertheless subject to sampling error because they
 are based on the area-specific sample data.
 The second part of the model thus assumes that the direct estimates are
 centered around the true poverty rates as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{d}=y_{i}^{a}+\omega_{i}\label{eq:2nd stage-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents the measurement error and is assumed to have heteroskedastic
 variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\omega,i}^{2}$
\end_inset

 because area sample sizes are often unequal.
 These variances are assumed to be known, although in practice they are
 estimated using the survey data.
 Given the two parts of the model, the survey data can then be used to obtain
 parameter estimates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The best linear unbiased predictor (BLUP) is obtained by predicting poverty
 rates through the model 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\hat{\beta}+\hat{\varepsilon}_{i}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\varepsilon}_{i}$
\end_inset

 represent the estimated coefficients and area-level effects, respectively.
 More specifically, the estimated area effect is 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\varepsilon}_{i}=\psi_{i}\left(y_{i}^{d}-x_{i}\hat{\beta}\right)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{i}=\hat{\sigma}_{\varepsilon}^{2}/\left(\hat{\sigma}_{\varepsilon}^{2}+\hat{\sigma}_{\omega,i}^{2}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 In essence, the final estimates for areas contained in the survey are the
 weighted average between the direct estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}$
\end_inset

 and the synthetic estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 where the weights, 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\psi_{i})$
\end_inset

, are determined by the quality of the model.
 For the non-sampled entities, the area-level model simply uses the synthetic
 estimator to produce predictions.
 A critical feature of the area-level model is that it is a direct competitor
 to the machine-learning approach because both methods have the same data
 requirements.
 Also similar to the machine-learning approach, the area-level model can
 be implemented at the PSU or municipality levels.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Estimates at the PSU level may be aggregated to a higher level.
 See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "rao2015small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for more details.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Regarding unit-level models, the well-known methods of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2003micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "molina2010small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 are based on the nested error model originally proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "battese1988asa"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This model assumes that (transformed) equivalized household level income
 or consumption relates linearly to a vector of household-level covariates
 according to the following model:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
w_{iv}=x_{iv}\theta+\mu_{i}+\xi_{iv}\label{eq:sae-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{iv}$
\end_inset

 denotes the transformed equivalized income of household 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 in location 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iv}$
\end_inset

 represents a vector of household-specific characteristics, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is a vector of coefficients on the household characteristics, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

 is location-specific random effect such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma_{\mu}^{2})$
\end_inset

 , and 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{iv}$
\end_inset

 is a household-level error term such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{iv}\sim N(0,\sigma_{\xi}^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The parameters of the model are typically estimated via restricted maximum
 likelihood or Henderson's Method III (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "henderson1953estimation"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Henderson's Method III is coupled with generalized least squares to obtain
 the regression coefficients.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Once the model is estimated, the parameters are then used to repeatedly
 generate income estimates for all households in a corresponding census
 dataset.
 That is, the income level of each household is predicted 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 times using the model estimates as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{{w}}_{ivk}=x_{iv}\hat{\theta}+\mu_{ik}^{*}+\xi_{ivk}^{*}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 indexes replications and where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{ik}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{ivk}^{*}$
\end_inset

 are drawn from their assumed distributions.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "rodas2021pull"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for a more detailed description.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 As a final step, the replicates can be used to calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 estimates of the poverty rate for each geographic entity and these estimates
 can then be averaged to arrive at a final prediction for each area.
 Note that there are multiple versions of the unit-level model, distinguished
 primarily through different treatments of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{ik}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{ivk}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 We use a particular version known as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

census empirical best
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 method, which is a variant of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

empirical best
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 method of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "molina2010small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
As opposed to empirical best estimates, census empirical best estimates
 do not include survey observations when calculating area-level poverty
 rates.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "rodas2021pull"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 show that when the sample size is small relative to the population, the
 difference between the two methods is negligible.
 For additional discussion, see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "molina2019desagregacion"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The key assumption of the unit-level model is that the distribution of the
 explanatory variables is similar between the survey used for estimation
 and the census used for prediction.
 This assumption is generally violated if the census is outdated – which
 is often the case in developing countries – and in this event the application
 of the unit-level model will lead to estimates which may be biased since
 the survey parameters are applied to a different population from which
 the survey was drawn from.
 Importantly, machine-learning approaches to poverty mapping are commonly
 motivated by the fact that censuses are outdated and are thus viewed as
 a way to produce timely poverty maps in a data-constrained environment.
 Stated differently, given the differing data requirements between the unit-leve
l model and the machine-learning approach, the two methods are not generally
 considered to be direct competitors.
 The unit-level model nevertheless serves as a useful performance benchmark,
 as it is often viewed as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

gold standard
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of poverty mapping.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike unit-level models, unit-context models were specifically developed
 to be used in off-census years.
 Initially developed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "nguyen2012method"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and further refined by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "masaki2020small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, these models are based on a simple modification of the unit-level approach:
 rather than modeling household-level income as a function of household-level
 characteristics, unit-context models instead use only area-level covariates
 from the census.
 Specifically, the unit-context model replaces the survey-based covariates
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iv}$
\end_inset

 in Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:sae-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) with census aggregates 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and thus relaxes the assumption that the survey-based covariates match
 the moments of those from the census.
 Like the area-level model, the unit-context model is a direct competitor
 to the machine-learning approach because it is feasible to implement with
 the same data.
 However, it is worth noting that the unit-context model has been shown
 to produce biased and noisy poverty estimates, largely due to its inability
 to explain between-household variation in income 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "corral2022guidelines"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Model Validation
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:validation"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712735950
The coefficient of determination or 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the most commonly used metric for validating poverty maps generated
 using machine-learning methods.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "jean2016combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pokhriyal2017combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "steele2017mapping"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "lee2020high"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yeh2020using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, or 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chi2022microestimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 In this section, we first critically assess this validation approach by
 discussing three ways in which the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 can be misleading in terms of model assessment.
 We then argue that a better alternative is to validate poverty estimates
 using the mean squared error, which is not subject to many of the limitations
 of the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Finally, we outline another validation procedure that focuses more directly
 on what is of ultimate concern with poverty mapping, namely informing the
 targeting of resources for the purposes of poverty alleviation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712735950
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 denote the actual or true poverty indicator for the 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{th}$
\end_inset

 geographical entity.
 Further, let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{p}$
\end_inset

 denote the direct estimate and model-based prediction of the poverty indicator,
 respectively.
 The standard method for assessing the predictive performance of model-based
 poverty estimates calculates the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 from a regression like the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712735950
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{d}=\alpha+\delta y_{i}^{p}+\zeta_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 are the regression coefficients and 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta$
\end_inset

 represents the error term.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that any regression of this form can only be estimated for the sampled
 regions with direct estimates.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 for the above regression can be written as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712735950
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{d}^{2}=\frac{\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{\zeta}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}\label{eq:r2direct}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of the direct estimate and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\zeta}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 thus conveys information about how much of the variance of the direct estimate
 is explained by the model-based prediction.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712735950
While unbiased, direct estimates can be imprecise estimates of true poverty
 indicators 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "rodas2021pull"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Ideally, one would assess the performance of model-based predictions on
 the basis of the true poverty indicators themselves.
 That is, model-based predictions are more appropriately assessed using
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 from the following regression:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712735950
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{a}=\alpha+\delta y_{i}^{p}+\upsilon_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\upsilon$
\end_inset

 represents the error term.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 for this regression can written as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712735950
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{a}^{2}=\frac{\sigma_{a}^{2}-\sigma_{\upsilon}^{2}}{\sigma_{a}^{2}}\label{eq:r2true}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of the true poverty indicator and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\upsilon}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\upsilon$
\end_inset

.
 We are specifically interested in characterizing the bias of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 when it is used to assess true quantity of interest, 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712735950
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}=y_{i}^{a}+\omega_{i}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 is a mean-zero error term with variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\omega}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 That is, we view this as a classical measurement error problem where the
 direct estimate of the poverty indicator is a noisy estimate of the true
 poverty indicator.
 Note that the preceding equation implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta_{i}=\upsilon_{i}+\omega_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\zeta}^{2}=\sigma_{\upsilon}^{2}+\sigma_{\omega}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "majeske2010quantifying"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, we then define the bias as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
B=R_{a}^{2}-R_{d}^{2}\,.\label{eq:bias}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Substituting Eqs.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:r2direct"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) and (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:r2true"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) into our expression for the bias, we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712735950
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
B=\frac{\sigma_{a}^{2}-\sigma_{\upsilon}^{2}}{\sigma_{a}^{2}}-\frac{\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{\upsilon}^{2}-\sigma_{\omega}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}=\frac{\sigma_{\omega}^{2}(\sigma_{a}^{2}-\sigma_{\upsilon}^{2})}{\sigma_{d}^{2}\sigma_{a}^{2}}=\frac{\sigma_{\omega}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}R_{a}^{2}\,.\label{eq:bias_res}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Finally, we can substitute Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:bias_res"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) into Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:bias"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) and rearrange, which gives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712735950
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{d}^{2}=R_{a}^{2}\left(\frac{\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{\omega}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}\right)\label{eq:relationship}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and establishes a basic relationship between 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "majeske2010quantifying"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for additional mathematical details.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712735950
In Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:relationship"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) above, we see that 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}=R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\omega}^{2}=0$
\end_inset

 or, equivalently, when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}=y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 In the presence of measurement error or when 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\omega}^{2}>0$
\end_inset

, we then see that 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 will be biased downward and the magnitude of the bias will be determined
 by the size of 
\begin_inset Formula $(\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{\omega}^{2})/\sigma_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Validation exercises based on 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 will thus tend to mischaracterize the performance of model-based approaches
 to poverty mapping, with the resulting estimates of 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 being biased downward.
 One implication of the above is that 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is context-dependent and will generally be influenced by the design of
 the survey data (e.g., larger sample sizes will tend to reduce measurement
 error, all else equal).
 In particular, a poorly performing model applied to a low bias setting
 may yield an estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 that is higher than an estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 from a strong model applied to a high bias setting.
 The presence of bias in the measures of 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 can thus reverse the rank ordering of models when comparisons are made
 across contexts.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yeh2020using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chi2022microestimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, or 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "aiken2022machine"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for this type of cross-context comparison of 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 values.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1712735950
There is an additional form of bias in 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 that can lead to overoptimistic conclusions related to model validity.
 To illustrate this, note that 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 in Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:r2true"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) is mathematically equivalent to the squared (Pearson) correlation coefficient
 between 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{p}$
\end_inset

.
 A well-known property of the correlation coefficient is that it is invariant
 to any positive affine transformation of its arguments, implying that the
 
\shape italic
squared
\shape default
 correlation coefficient is invariant to 
\shape italic
any
\shape default
 affine transformation 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "gujarati2003basic"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
More specifically, the correlation coefficient is invariant to any 
\shape italic
positive
\shape default
 affine transformation because it changes signs when one of its arguments
 is scaled by some negative constant.
 The 
\shape italic
squared
\shape default
 correlation coefficient is then invariant to any affine transformation.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 We can thus write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{a}^{2}(y_{i}^{a},y_{i}^{p})=R_{a}^{2}(y_{i}^{a},q_{1}+q_{2}y_{i}^{p})\label{eq:shift}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{2}$
\end_inset

 are some arbitrary constants.
 The implication of the above is that the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 is insensitive to systematic bias in the model-based poverty estimates.
 For example, consider some unbiased poverty estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{u}$
\end_inset

, meaning that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}^{u}-y_{i}^{a})=0.$
\end_inset

 Now consider another estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{b}$
\end_inset

 that is identical to 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{u}$
\end_inset

 up to some locational shift, implying that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{b}=y_{i}^{u}+q$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}^{b}-y_{i}^{a})=q$
\end_inset

.
 While 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{b}$
\end_inset

 is biased (i.e., it will systematically over- or under-estimate poverty),
 it will achieve an 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 that is identical to 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{u}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1713115820
A final issue related to 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 as a performance metric is that it is affected by the variance of the true
 poverty measures.
 To see this, consider the following re-expression of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{a}^{2}=1-\frac{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}(y_{i}^{a}-\alpha-\delta y_{i}^{p})^{2}}{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}(y_{i}^{a}-\bar{{y}}^{a})^{2}}=1-\frac{\text{{MSE}}(y_{i}^{a},\alpha+\delta y_{i}^{p})}{\text{{Var}}(y_{i}^{a})}\label{eq:variance}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is the number of geographical entities.
 The above shows that 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 can be viewed as a normalized version of the mean squared error with the
 variance of the true poverty indicator used as the normalization factor.
 This approach to normalization produces an additional source of context-depende
nce in 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Specifically, consider two models that perform equally well in that they
 achieve the same mean squared error.
 However, assume that one model is applied to a context where the variance
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 is large and the other is applied to a low variance context.
 The above then implies that the model applied to the high variance context
 will achieve a higher 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

, even though the performance of the two models is arguably identical.
 
\change_inserted 428888767 1713115822

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_inserted 428888767 1713117966
The different methods for poverty mapping are assessed on how well they
 predict the true poverty rates acorss all municipalities.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\change_inserted 428888767 1713116289
The pseudo census created represents our population and their reported incomes
 allow for the calculation of the true poverty rates within each municipality.
\change_unchanged

\end_layout

\end_inset

 Estimates are evaluated on how biased these are and the over all Mean Squared
 Error (MSE).
 Note that because the goal is to obtain poverty estimates for each municipality
 these measures are calculated for every area.
 Model bias and MSE are approximated empirically as in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "molina2010small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The bias and MSE for each area 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 are calculated across 
\begin_inset Formula $N=500$
\end_inset

 sets of estimates obtained across each of the 500 samples taken from the
 population using each of the proposed methods, 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

.
 Specifically, the bias of mehtod 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 in area 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is calculated as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_inserted 428888767 1713119572
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Bias\left(\hat{y}_{i}^{j}\right)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\left(\hat{y}_{i,n}^{j}-y_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_inserted 428888767 1713119602
The MSE is calculated as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_inserted 428888767 1713119686
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
MSE\left(\hat{y}_{i}^{j}\right)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\left(\hat{y}_{i,n}^{j}-y_{i}\right)^{2}\label{eq:mse}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_inserted 428888767 1713119629

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\change_deleted 428888767 1713119718
Rather than relying on 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 as a performance metric, one can avoid regression-based model assessment
 altogether by calculating the mean squared error directly:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\text{{MSE}}(y_{i}^{a},y_{i}^{p})=\frac{{1}}{N}\sum_{i}(y_{i}^{a}-y_{i}^{p})^{2}\,,\label{eq:mse}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which avoids the normalization issue and provides a more direct understanding
 of the expected squared error of the estimates.
 
\change_unchanged
The mean squared error in Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:mse"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) is sensitive to systematic biases in the predictions.
 Reconsidering the biased and unbiased poverty estimates described above
 (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{u}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{b}$
\end_inset

), we can write 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{{MSE}}(y_{i}^{b},y_{i}^{a})=\text{{MSE}}(y_{i}^{u}+q,y_{i}^{a})=\text{{MSE}}(y_{i}^{u},y_{i}^{a})+q^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Systematic biases in the estimates will thus be penalized by the mean squared
 error.
 Further, with multiple estimates for each entity, one can calculate entity-spec
ific mean squared errors, which can be decomposed into parts associated
 with the bias and variance of the estimates.
 Where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{ik}^{p}$
\end_inset

 denotes estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1,2,\ldots,K$
\end_inset

 for the 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{th}$
\end_inset

 entity, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\text{{MSE}}_{i}=\frac{{1}}{K}\sum_{k}(y_{i}^{a}-y_{ik}^{p})^{2}=\frac{{1}}{K}\sum_{k}(y_{ik}^{p}-\bar{{y}}_{i}^{p})^{2}+(\bar{{y}}_{i}^{p}-y_{i}^{a})^{2}\label{eq:mseg}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the first term on the right-hand side of the above captures the variance
 of the estimates for entity 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and the second term captures the (squared) bias.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "hastie2009elements"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for derivation and detailed discussion of the bias-variance decomposition.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The primary reason for obtaining granular poverty estimates is to improve
 the targeting of resources.
 While understanding the error of the poverty estimates is useful in this
 regard, the ultimate concern is to understand how different methodological
 choices affect poverty alleviation by influencing the efficiency of targeting.
 Consequently, we also consider how machine-learning methods perform in
 terms of reducing poverty in the context of the census created from the
 Mexican Intercensal Survey.
 Similar to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2007poverty"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, we propose simulating a hypothetical poverty-alleviation program that
 distributes resources to households according to a given set of small area
 estimates.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yeh2020using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chi2022microestimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "aiken2022machine"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for examples of other studies that have conducted targeting simulations.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 In addition to the estimates, the procedure requires true household-level
 incomes from a census-type dataset.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The procedure we use in our simulations is as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace -25bp
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\paragraph_spacing single
\noindent
Using the true household-level incomes, calculate the poverty headcount
 and poverty gap for the country as a whole for some chosen poverty line.
 Using the poverty gap measure, determine the budget required to completely
 eradicate poverty.
 Calculate an individual-level transfer amount by dividing the total budget
 by the number of people living in poverty.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\paragraph_spacing single
\noindent
Using a given set of small area estimates, order the municipalities according
 to their estimated poverty headcounts, from most to least poor.
 For the poorest municipality, augment the true per capita income of all
 households by an amount equal to the transfer (i.e., each household receives
 an amount equal to the transfer times the number of household members).
 Continue this distribution procedure for the second poorest municipality,
 the third poorest municipality, and so on until there are not enough resources
 to cover all households in a given municipality.
 For the marginal municipality, distribute the remaining budget equally
 among individuals in that municipality.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\paragraph_spacing single
\noindent
Using the post-transfer household incomes, calculate an updated poverty
 headcount to assess the effects of the distribution scheme on poverty alleviati
on.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Repeat steps 2 and 3 for each of the 500 sets of small area estimates generated
 by each method under each survey sample.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace -10bp
\end_inset

The benchmark poverty alleviation from this scheme corresponds to the results
 of applying the above to the true municipal poverty rankings.
 The results for each method are compared to that result.
 In our implementation, we fix the poverty line at the 25th percentile of
 per capita income prior to transfers.
 The benchmark poverty rate, after delivering transfers to people in municipalit
ies ranked by their true poverty rate, is 19.9 percent.
\end_layout

