\begin_layout Standard
In Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:validation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, we discussed two other ways that the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 can be misleading for assessing model performance: it is invariant to locationa
l shifts and influenced by the variance of the true poverty rates.
 Using the Mexican data, we can assess the relevance of the former issue
 by comparing the true 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 to a constrained version of the true 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 that fixes the intercept and coefficient to zero and one, respectively.
 By constraining the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 in this manner, we remove its ability to adjust for systematic differences
 between the poverty estimates and the true poverty rates.
 Panel (b) of Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:r2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 presents the results and we see that, as expected, the unconstrained 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 overstates the model's performance, albeit only to a small degree.
 We nevertheless find that even these small differences can lead to model
 selection issues.
 In particular, the constrained 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 identifies the PSU level as the appropriate level of estimation for 355
 of the 500 samples whereas the unconstrained version, as mentioned, selects
 the PSU level for 437 out of the 500 samples.
 The commonly used unconstrained 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 then selects the incorrect model for 82 of the 500 samples.
\end_layout

