\begin_layout Standard
The performance of machine learning appears to depend heavily on the covariates
 used in the model, with census-based covariates considerably improving
 model performance.
 Another way to see that the census-based covariates have greater predictive
 power than the remotely-sensed covariates is by looking at feature importance.
 One popular measure of feature importance summarizes the gain (i.e., reduction
 of the loss function) associated with any given feature.
 That is, for a given run of the model, feature importance captures the
 average gain of a given feature, where the gains are averaged across all
 trees in the model.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

